## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about multi-word bit manipulations and safe 64→128-bit extension without loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit reversal via two 64-bit reversals and swapping halves
- Parallel-bits reversal (mask/shifts)
- Endianness-insensitive mapping of bit indices

## Code Example
```javascript
function reverse64(x) {
  x = (x >> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n | (x & 0x5555555555555555n) << 1n;
  x = (x >> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n | (x & 0x3333333333333333n) << 2n;
  x = (x >> 4n) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) << 4n;
  x = (x >> 8n) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) << 8n;
  x = (x >> 16n) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) << 16n;
  x = (x >> 32n) | (x << 32n);
  return x;
}
function reverse128(hi, lo) {
  const revLo = reverse64(lo);
  const revHi = reverse64(hi);
  return { hi: revLo, lo: revHi };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to streaming 128-bit values?
- How to verify correctness with random tests?

## Why This Is Asked

Parity computation over 128 bits tests the ability to design constant-time reductions using only bitwise operations and shifts, without loops or branches.

## Key Concepts
- Bitwise XOR folding across 128 bits
- Progressive reduction by shifting (64,32,16,8,4,2,1)
- Final extraction with a single bit AND

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128_t;
unsigned parity128(uint128_t x) {
  uint64_t v = x.hi ^ x.lo;
  v ^= v >> 32;
  v ^= v >> 16;
  v ^= v >> 8;
  v ^= v >> 4;
  v ^= v >> 2;
  v ^= v >> 1;
  return v & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to 256 bits while preserving the same approach?
- Can you prove the operation is constant time regardless of input bit distribution?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to derive circular adjacency via rotation, and to implement a 128-bit popcount using SWAR style reductions; ensures understanding of cross-boundary bit relationships.

## Key Concepts

- Circular adjacency using ROTL
- 128-bit representation as two 64-bit halves
- SWAR/popcount with bitwise masks and shifts

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline uint128 rotl128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = (x.hi << 1) | (x.lo >> 63);
  r.lo = (x.lo << 1) | (x.hi >> 63);
  return r;
}

static inline unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x){
  uint64_t a = x.hi;
  uint64_t b = x.lo;
  a = a - ((a >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  a = (a & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((a >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  a = (a + (a >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  unsigned int ca = (a * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;

  b = b - ((b >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  b = (b & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((b >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  b = (b + (b >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  unsigned int cb = (b * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;

  return ca + cb;
}
```

Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to non-power-of-two widths?
- What are performance implications on vectorized lanes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests bitwise reasoning and ensures a constant-time, loop-free parity computation relevant to integrity checks.

## Key Concepts
- XOR-fold parity reductions without loops
- Endianness-neutral bit tricks; GCC/Clang optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
unsigned parity64(uint64_t x) {
  x ^= x >> 32;
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  x ^= x >> 2;
  x ^= x >> 1;
  return x & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
